1.d4 Nf6 2.c4 g6 3.Nc3 Bg7 4.e4 d6 5.f3 O-O 6.Bg5 c5 7.d5 a6 8.a4 h6 9.Be3 e6
10.Qd2 exd5 11.cxd5 Re8 12.Nge2 Nbd7 13.Nd1 Ne5 14.Nec3 g5 15.Be2 Nh5 16.O-O Qf6
17.a5 Nf4 18.Na4 Bd7 19.Ndc3
{ Critical moment: Black to move. In the historical DCC snapshot, Bxa4 and Rad8 started tied at -0.10 and DCC preferred Bxa4 after a ten-half-move CDB trajectory. The newer deep review does not reproduce Bxa4 as a unique best move: fresh CDB favors Nxe2+/Rad8 among scored moves, while local Stockfish d36 has Nxe2+ slightly ahead of Bxa4/Rad8. Keep this as a dated DCC-method example, not proof of superiority. }
19... Bxa4 20.Rxa4
{ Critical moment: 20...Reb8 was BD's independent positional idea: support the b-pawn with the e-rook while keeping the a-rook available. Fresh CDB supports Reb8 near equality, and local Stockfish d34 scores Reb8 0.00 versus the played Rac8 -0.42 from Black's perspective. The same plan appears in CDB continuations with ...b6 or ...b5 and rook pressure on the b-file. }
20... Rac8 21.Kh1 c4 22.Rb4
{ Critical moment: Candidate quality and final choice separate here. BD considered Qd8, Re7, Qe7 and Rb8, then chose Rb8. Deep local Stockfish d32 instead ranks Rc7 0.00, Ned3 -0.09, Re7 -0.12, Qd8 -0.57, Qe7 -1.26, played Nxe2 -1.35, and Rb8 -2.37. The lesson is not that history was automatically best: BD's candidate set contained better moves than both final choices. }
22... Nxe2 23.Qxe2 Qe7 24.Rb6 Nd3 25.Qd2 f5 26.Bg1
{ Critical moment: Both human ideas focus on the f-pawn, but the strongest family in this review is quieter. CDB has Rf8 and Rc7 at 0.00; deep local Stockfish d33 gives Rf8 0.00, f4 -0.90 and fxe4 -1.19 for Black. This is a candidate-generation lesson: before choosing between two related pawn moves, search for a different type of improving move. }
26... f4 27.b3 Ne5 28.Rb4 cxb3 29.Rxb3
{ Critical moment: BD saw both Nc4 and Qc7 but chose Qc7. CDB and Stockfish support Nc4; local SF d28 gives Nc4 +1.00 versus Qc7 -0.65 for Black. The difference is move order: Nc4 attacks the queen on d2 with tempo, so playing the queen move first gives White time that the knight move would have taken away. }
29... Nc4 30.Qa2 Qc7 31.e5
{ Critical moment: BD generated Rxe5 before seeing the evaluation but chose Bxe5, matching the game. Fresh CDB scores Rxe5 +1.73; local Stockfish d32 gives Rxe5 +1.98 versus Bxe5 +1.40 for Black. This is a candidate-ranking miss, not a candidate-generation miss: the stronger move was already in the human set. }
31... Bxe5 32.Ne4
{ Critical moment: Black can take a5 with either queen or knight, and BD saw both. Fresh CDB gives Qxa5 +2.73; local Stockfish d31 gives Qxa5 +2.90 versus Nxa5 -0.07 for Black. The tactical reason appears after Nxa5: 33.Bb6 attacks both Qc7 and Na5. Taking with the queen keeps Nc4 controlling b6 and avoids that double target. }
32... Nxa5 33.Rbb1 Nc4 34.Rfc1
{ Critical moment: This is the largest missed black opportunity in the review. Fresh CDB scores Qa5 +3.93; local Stockfish d36 gives Qa5 +4.18 versus the played b5 +0.26. Qa5 attacks White's queen on a2 with tempo while Nc4 still controls b6; a CDB continuation then finds 35.Qb3 Qxd5. }
34... b5 35.Qxa6 Qf7 36.Qxb5
{ Critical moment: 36...Na3! attacks both Qb5 and Rb1. BD found it in about 15 seconds and Kasparov played it. This is a clean tactical motif rather than an authority claim: one knight move gains tempo on the queen while creating a second material threat against the rook. }
36... Na3 37.Qa6
{ Critical moment: The immediate captures both fail. Local Stockfish d33 gives Ra8 +3.27, Nxb1 -4.59 and Rxc1 -7.00 for Black. Ra8 solves the position because it moves the c8 rook out of the tactic and attacks Qa6 with tempo; only after the queen moves does ...Nxb1 become safe. }
37... Ra8 38.Qd3 Nxb1 39.Rxb1 Re7 40.h3 Qg6 41.Kh2
{ Critical moment: BD wanted h5 one move before Kasparov. Here it is not merely the same plan early: CDB scores h5 +4.23, and local Stockfish d36 gives h5 +5.36 versus the played Rf8 +4.06 for Black. The move supports the g-pawn advance and makes the route toward the white king more concrete. }
41... Rf8 42.Rb6 h5 43.Qa6 g4 44.Nxd6 gxf3 45.Qf1
{ Critical moment: BD chose fxg2 while Kasparov played Bxd6. Both are winning in the stored deep search, but local Stockfish finds 45...fxg2 as M14 versus 45...Bxd6 at +9.32. One verified line starts 46.Qf3 Qd3 47.Nf5 Qxf3 and eventually reaches ...Qh2#. This is the clearest late-game case where the alternative is supported by a concrete forced mating line, not merely by a slightly higher evaluation. }
45... Bxd6 46.gxf3 Rg7 47.Bf2 Qf6 48.h4 Rg6 49.Qh3 Qf5 50.Rc6  0-1

